{"id": "ocarina_of_crime_why_zelda_is_able", "source_key": "thegamebusiness", "source": "thegamebusiness.com", "section": "deep_analysis", "title": "Ocarina of Crime: Why Zelda is able to escape GTA 6’s shadow", "url": "https://www.thegamebusiness.com/p/ocarina-of-crime-why-zelda-is-able", "published_at": "2026-10-19T16:47:23", "author": "Christopher Dring", "excerpt": "Here’s The Dring! GTA is already dominating the conversation. Plus, what to make of all the AI noise?", "text": "The Game Business Show\nOcarina of Crime: Why Zelda is able to escape GTA 6’s shadow\n0:00\nCurrent time: 0:00 / Total time: -13:52\n-13:52\nPaid episode\nThe full episode is only available to paid subscribers of The Game Business\nOcarina of Crime: Why Zelda is able to escape GTA 6’s shadow\nHere’s The Dring! GTA is already dominating the conversation. Plus, what to make of all the AI noise?\nChristopher Dring\n15 hours ago\n∙ Paid\nHello there!\nI’ve had quite the week. On Thursday, I travelled to Middlesbrough in the UK for the New Horizons conference. Middlesbrough is a town in Yorkshire where you’ll find a couple of notable game developers, including Minecraft Dungeons studio Double Eleven, plus the UK office of Dead by Daylight developer Behaviour.\nNevertheless, it’s not where you’d expect to find a big games conference. And yet, New Horizons has attracted some pretty big names. Including that of ‘father of PlayStation’ Ken Kutaragi, who I had the privilege of interviewing on stage at the event.\nNow, I’d met Ken before, and my Spidey senses were telling me that this was going to be a tricky chat. Ken speaks great English and understands it well, but it’s not his native language. And I struggled to follow some of his answers. In fact, at one point, Ken asked me a direct question that I couldn’t decipher. I am still not entirely sure what it was.\nPicture by Andy Robinson\nThat’s the challenge with live, on-stage interviews. There’s no ability to make edits or corrections in post, or even stop the recording to make some suggestions. You’re on the rollercoaster, and you just have to strap in.\nI was following a lot more by the end of the session, and it was genuinely insightful (and quite far out there at points). Certainly, some people in the audience were following along, laughing and clapping at the right moments.\nI was planning to put the interview out this week for you, but we will need a little bit longer to sort out the subtitles and see if we need any dubbing.\nI’m looking forward to hearing what he had to say as much as you are!\nGTA 6 is already sucking the oxygen out of the room\nThe Game Business Show\nChristopher Dring hosts interviews, in-depth analysis and special features on what's truly going on in the video games industry.\nChristopher Dring hosts interviews, in-depth analysis and special features on what's truly going on in the video games industry.\nListen on\nSubstack App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nYouTube\nAppears in episode\nChristopher Dring\nRecent Episodes\nFixing Call of Duty: Inside Activision’s Modern Warfare 4 comeback plan\nOct 15   •   Christopher Dring\nStar Fox developer Velan: How to survive as a North American game studio\nOct 13   •   Christopher Dring\nIs prompt-based gaming a bad thing? And is there anything new to Xbox XP?\nOct 12   •   Christopher Dring\nHow did PS5 boxed games sell in September? And the problem with industry consolidation\nOct 8   •   Christopher Dring  and  Joost van Dreunen\nHow Undead Labs saved itself and State of Decay from oblivion\nOct 6   •   Christopher Dring\nIs there more to the MMO resurgence than just nostalgia?\nOct 5   •   Christopher Dring\nGames media crisis deepens, Bloober hunts weird horror titles, and New York Games Week soft launches\nOct 1   •   Christopher Dring", "html": "<div class=\"visibility-check\"></div><div><div class=\"shows-post-audio-player-wrapper-outer player-wrapper-outer-eAOhcD\"><div class=\"shows-post-audio-player-wrapper-inner player-wrapper-inne